# Per-path token ceilings, gated by `itok check` (pinned tier, V5). Only
# registered paths are checked; everything else is unchecked (V10). This is
# itok checking itself -- the dogfood.
#
# WHAT THIS CEILING IS: a cap on ENTRY COST -- what a session loads before
# any work begins. Measured: overhead (system prompt + tool schemas)
# ~28,543 itok + SPEC.md ~26,532 = ~55,075 resident at turn one, 42% of
# gpt-oss:20b's 131,072 window and 28% of a 200k one. Entry cost is
# re-billed EVERY turn (V84/V94), so it is subtracted from headroom before
# the agent does anything. Not a fit constraint: SPEC.md is 2.5% of 1M.
#
# HEADROOM is a DIFFERENT quantity and not a synonym for the slack below:
# headroom is what is LEFT after everything is loaded (window minus used),
# which is what `itok headroom` reports (V91). Slack under this ceiling is
# maintenance pressure. Conflating them borrows a verb's precise meaning
# for a place it does not apply -- V2, inside our own vocabulary.
#
# It forces a REVIEW, not a shrink (V84). Set with real slack (~10-13%): a
# ceiling a hair above current turns every legitimate addition into a
# raise, and a threshold raised reflexively trains the reflex instead of
# catching the problem (V71). It catches RUNAWAY growth; T70's compaction
# machine pays the debt -- two jobs, and conflating them made the ceiling
# useless at both.
#
# History: 19,542 -> 20,500 (T49) -> 21,500 -> 25,000 -> 27,500 -> 31,000
# -> 34,000 -> 36,000. Seven raises, every addition correct: the spec grows
# because the project does (103 invariants). Why each raise was justified
# lives in its commit, not here (V26).
#
# The sixth covered FIVE bug records in one day -- B17 (the published
# .crate failing its own suite), B18 (a merge commit read as empty), B19
# (`-C` is not isolation), B20 (a coverage badge no machine could
# reproduce) and B21 (hk silent after a failed step). Section B is
# APPEND-ONLY by nature, so a fixed ceiling over it is a promise to
# re-review at a fixed interval rather than a cap that can hold; that is
# the ceiling working, not failing.
#
# The SEVENTH corrects the sixth rather than absorbing new content, and it
# is recorded that way on purpose. 34,000 was set against 31,586 -- ~7.1%
# slack, UNDER the 10-13% this file asks for two paragraphs above. The
# shortfall was noticed and shipped anyway, which is the rule being read
# and then not applied. 36,000 against 32,033 is ~11%, inside the band.
#
# Worth keeping as the lesson: a ceiling set below its own slack rule is
# the "fires on the next legitimate sentence" failure the rule exists to
# prevent (V71), and B17 landing a day later would have fired it. The rule
# was right and the number was wrong; the fix is the number.
#
# The fifth was REVIEWED, not reflexive, and the review shed 855 chars
# before it moved the number: T90 and T91 described machinery that no
# longer exists (`cavekit-spec`, the ported guard), and V26 says method
# lives in the commit trail, so they trimmed to what+cites. The raise
# covers what remained -- V104 and B14, a new invariant class with its bug
# record, which is the spec growing because the project did.
#
# 31,000 against 27,724 is ~12% slack, per the rule above. The previous
# ceiling had drifted to 242, which is not a ceiling: it fires on the next
# legitimate sentence and trains the reflex it exists to catch (V71).
#
# README 4k -> 5k, ONE reviewed raise covering a documented set, not six
# reflexive bumps as each section lands. What it buys, measured: the
# LLM-provenance block (494), then `Why`, `Guarantees`, `Status`, `Use it as
# a library` and `The name` -- the sections a first visitor actually reads,
# and the ones this README lacked while going public. `Status` in particular
# is where V107's publication-is-not-a-rung reasoning reaches someone who
# will never open the spec.
#
# This is the ceiling working as designed (V84): it fired, the addition was
# justified item by item, and the number moved once with the reason in the
# commit rather than here (V26). A README that grows because the project went
# public is the project growing; a README that grows every week is not, and
# the next raise should be asked to prove which it is.
SPEC.md    36000
README.md  5k
